Subject: [PATCH 7/7] docs: five seeds overturn the single-seed reading -- the
 gap is completion and safety

Three of my conclusions this session came from one seed and all three were
wrong. On seed block 262000 with five episodes, and with the MPC on its own
best weighting (terminal_weight 1000, not weakened):

  Oracle (offline, truth)   5/5 done  5/5 clean  worst +0.148  dv 1.844
  MPC h20 (0.82x budget)    3/5 done  3/5 clean  worst -0.337  dv 1.932
  MPC h50 (1.95x budget)    4/5 done  4/5 clean  worst -0.368  dv 2.247

Every failure is corridor_lateral: the chaser crosses the 6 m latch before it
has aligned with the body-fixed approach axis sweeping at 0.0412 rad/s, the
terminal corridor goes live, and it is already outside the cone. Aligning takes
about 5 s; h20 sees that boundary 0.30 m ahead and h50 1.50 m ahead.

Quadrupling the horizon does not fix it. h50 still fails the same seed the same
way at 1.95x the control period, and the one extra completion is bought by
flying on the boundary -- the worst margin among its successful episodes falls
from +0.145 to +0.018, eight times thinner.

Propellant is not the lever after all: over completed episodes MPC is 1.932
against the oracle's 1.844. The fuel-budget task element I argued for in the
previous revision would have addressed a gap that does not exist, and is
withdrawn.

n=5, so 3/5 against 4/5 is one seed and not a difference. What is stable is
that both horizons fail the same seed on the same constraint and that the
margin degrades monotonically with horizon. Twenty episodes are still required
before any of this is reported upward.

